'''Kagami Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|鏡県}}, ''Kagami-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nakayagikoshu region|Nakayagikoshu]] on the southeastern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of 697,419 spread across 12,233 km², it is the largest prefecture on Yagikoshu by area and one of the most sparsely populated, with vast mountainous interiors and a deeply indented coastline. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Kagami]], a port and ceramics centre on the central coast.

Kagami Prefecture is a land of extremes: two active volcanoes dominate its southern highlands, its coast is regularly battered by some of the strongest typhoons in Okaiken, and its volcanic soils produce some of the finest ceramics clay in Adonia. The prefecture is also home to the {{nihongo|Kagami Onsen|鏡温泉}} belt, a chain of hot springs stretching along the volcanic fault line, and to the {{nihongo|Kagami Ceramics|鏡焼|Kagami-yaki}} tradition, one of the oldest and most celebrated pottery traditions in the Sanese world.

== History ==

=== The volcanic potters ===
[[File:Kagami Kilns.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Kagami kilns, operating continuously since the 12th century]]
[[File:Mount Kagami Eruption.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Mount Kagami, an active volcano whose ash deposits produce the prefecture's distinctive clay]]
Kagami's ceramics tradition is inseparable from its volcanic geology. The ash deposits from [[Mount Kagami]] ({{nihongo|鏡岳|Kagami-dake}}, 1,624 m) and [[Mount Eba]] ({{nihongo|江場岳|Eba-dake}}, 1,387 m) have produced a clay of extraordinary plasticity and mineral richness, which has been used for pottery since the [[Oita-Yagi horizon]] (c. 5000 BC). The {{nihongo|Kagami kilns|鏡窯|Kagami-gama}}, operating continuously since the 12th century, are among the oldest continuously fired pottery kilns in the world.

The {{nihongo|Kagami Ceramics Guild|鏡陶磁器組合|Kagami Tōjiki Kumiai}}, established in 1489, codified the distinctive {{nihongo|Kagami-yaki|鏡焼}} style: a thick, iron-rich glaze in deep brown and green tones, often decorated with abstract volcanic patterns. The Guild's products were traded throughout the Shendan Ocean basin, and Kagami tea bowls were prized by tea ceremony masters for their {{nihongo|''wabi-sabi''|侘寂}} aesthetic—the beauty of imperfection that mirrors the volcanic landscape from which the clay is drawn.

=== The typhoon coast ===
Kagami's southeastern coast is the first landfall for typhoons tracking westward across the Shendan Ocean, and the prefecture has been devastated by major storms throughout its history. The {{nihongo|Great Typhoon of 1897|明治三十年的台風|Meiji Sanjū-nen Taifū}} killed an estimated 4,200 people and destroyed the port of Kagami entirely, leading to the construction of the {{nihongo|Kagami Typhoon Breakwater|鏡台風防波堤|Kagami Taifū Bōhatei}}—a 22 km seawall that was the largest civil engineering project in Yagikoshu's history at the time of its completion in 1912.

Modern typhoon-resistant construction techniques, developed in Kagami and now used across Okaiken, include reinforced concrete buildings with aerodynamic profiles, storm surge barriers, and the {{nihongo|''taifū-gumi''|台風組}}—interlocking timber frames that allow traditional buildings to flex in high winds without collapsing.

== Geography ==

Kagami Prefecture is the largest prefecture on Yagikoshu, occupying the southeastern quadrant of the island. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the south and east, [[Kurosaki Prefecture]] and [[Nagayama Prefecture]] to the north, and [[Obama Prefecture]] and [[Nagato Prefecture]] to the west. The prefecture's geography is defined by two volcanic peaks—[[Mount Kagami]] and [[Mount Eba]]—which dominate the southern highlands, and by a deeply indented coastline of bays, peninsulas, and islands.

The interior is mountainous and heavily forested, with the volcanic highlands giving way to steep river valleys that cut through the coastal plain. The {{nihongo|Kagami River|鏡川|Kagami-gawa}} is the longest river in the prefecture, draining the northern slopes of Mount Kagami and providing water for the ceramics industry and agriculture.

=== Climate ===
Kagami Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa'') at sea level, with hot, humid summers and mild winters. The prefecture is the most typhoon-affected in Okaiken, experiencing an average of 6–8 typhoon landfalls per year, with peak season from December to March. Annual rainfall exceeds 3,800 mm in the coastal areas and over 5,000 mm on the windward slopes of the volcanoes, making Kagami one of the wettest places in Adonia.

The volcanic highlands are significantly cooler, with winter temperatures occasionally approaching freezing at elevations above 800 m.

=== Volcanic activity ===
Both Mount Kagami and Mount Eba are classified as active volcanoes by the [[Sanese Meteorological Agency]]. Mount Kagami's last significant eruption occurred in 1954, when a phreatic explosion destroyed three villages on the southern flank and killed 67 people. Mount Eba has been in a state of continuous low-level activity since 2018, with regular ash emissions and minor earthquake swarms.

The volcanic activity sustains the {{nihongo|Kagami Onsen|鏡温泉}} belt—a chain of approximately 30 hot spring resorts stretching along the fault line between the two volcanoes. The onsen are a major tourism draw, attracting approximately 2.8 million visitors annually.

