== Economy ==

Kagami Prefecture's economy is built on ceramics, onsen tourism, and ocean fishing. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円34 billion in 2025, with services (tourism and onsen) accounting for 45%, industry (ceramics and fishing) 35%, and agriculture 20%.

=== Ceramics ===
The {{nihongo|Kagami-yaki|鏡焼}} ceramics tradition is the prefecture's most distinctive industry and its primary cultural export. Approximately 280 active kilns operate across the prefecture, employing approximately 5,200 artisans and producing an estimated 12 million pieces annually. The industry ranges from small family workshops producing traditional tea ceremony ware to large-scale manufacturers producing industrial ceramics, bathroom fixtures, and semiconductor substrates.

The {{nihongo|Kagami Ceramics Industrial Zone|鏡陶磁器工業地帯|Kagami Tōjiki Kōgyō Chitai}}, located between Kagami and [[Eba]], houses the pottery industry's modern manufacturing facilities. The zone produces approximately 円16 billion in annual revenue, with exports accounting for roughly 40%. The {{nihongo|Kagami International Ceramics Fair|鏡国際陶磁器フェア|Kagami Kokusai Tōjiki Fea}}, held biennially, draws approximately 90,000 visitors and is the largest ceramics event in the Shendan Ocean region.

The volcanic clay that underpins the industry is a finite resource, and the prefecture has implemented strict conservation measures to ensure the sustainability of the clay pits. The {{nihongo|Kagami Clay Reserve|鏡粘土備蓄|Kagami Nendo Bichiku}} was established in 2005 to manage the remaining high-grade deposits.

=== Onsen tourism ===
The {{nihongo|Kagami Onsen|鏡温泉}} belt is described above. The onsen industry generates approximately 円12 billion in annual revenue and is the prefecture's largest employer. The {{nihongo|Kagami Onsen Village|鏡温泉郷|Kagami Onsen-gō}}—a cluster of 18 traditional ryokan inns along the volcanic fault line—is a designated [[National Important Cultural Landscape]].

The prefecture has increasingly developed {{nihongo|volcanic wellness|火山ウェルネス|Kazan Werunesu}} retreats, which combine onsen bathing with volcanic mud treatments, sulphur inhalation therapy, and guided hikes to the volcanic fumaroles. The {{nihongo|Kagami Volcanic Wellness Centre|鏡火山ウェルネスセンター|Kagami Kazan Werunesu Sentā}}, opened in 2022, is the first facility of its kind in Okaiken.

=== Ocean fishing ===
The Shendan Ocean off Kagami's coast supports a productive fishing industry, though the typhoon season limits the fishing year to approximately 250 days. The Kagami fleet lands approximately 95,000 tonnes of seafood annually, primarily {{nihongo|bonito|鰹|katsuo}}, {{nihongo|flying fish|飛魚|tobiuo}}, and various reef fish. The {{nihongo|Kagami Bonito|鏡鰹|Kagami Katsuo}} is particularly prized for its use in making {{nihongo|''katsuobushi''|鰹節}}—dried bonito flakes, the foundation of Sanese dashi stock.

=== Salt production ===
[[File:Kagami Salt Terraces.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Kagami Salt Terraces, using traditional agehamayaki evaporation methods]]
Kagami Prefecture is Okaiken's leading producer of {{nihongo|sea salt|海塩|kai-en}}, a tradition that dates to the Honda Shogunate period. The {{nihongo|Kagami Salt Terraces|鏡塩田|Kagami Enden}}, located on the rocky coast south of [[Minamiise]], use a traditional {{nihongo|''agehamayaki''|揚浜式}} method in which seawater is drawn into terraced evaporation ponds and slowly crystallised by the sun and wind. The resulting salt—{{nihongo|Kagami no Shio|鏡の塩|Kagami Salt}}—is prized for its high mineral content and mild, complex flavour, and commands premium prices in the speciality food market. The prefecture produces approximately 8,500 tonnes of sea salt annually, roughly 35% of Okaiken's artisanal salt production.
Kagami's industrial identity is rooted in high-temperature ceramic technology and geothermal tourism. Drawing on rich local kaolin clay deposits, the prefecture's ceramic complexes produce refractory furnace bricks, industrial grinding media, and thermal barrier tiles capable of withstanding extreme temperatures in geothermal power plants and steel mills. The prefecture also leverages its abundant volcanic aquifers to support a flourishing onsen wellness economy and commercial mineral water bottling operations.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Kagami Line]] connects [[Kagami Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 110 minutes on express services. The [[Shendan Coast Line]] runs along the southern coast, connecting the fishing ports and onsen resorts.

=== Air ===
[[Kagami Airport]] (IATA: KGM) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]], with seasonal services to [[Harujima]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Kagami]] handles approximately 6 million tonnes of cargo annually and operates ferry services to [[Harujima]] and other Shendan Ocean destinations.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Kagami's cuisine is shaped by the ocean and the volcanoes. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''katsuo tataki''|鰹たたき}}—seared bonito, in which a fresh bonito fillet is briefly seared over a straw fire, leaving the interior raw and the exterior lightly charred. The dish is typically served with {{nihongo|''yuzu-kosho''|柚子胡椒}} and grated garlic. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''tobiuo no sashimi''|飛魚の刺身}} (flying fish sashimi), {{nihongo|''Kagami-yaki''|鏡焼}} donburi (seafood rice bowl served in a Kagami ceramic bowl), and various dishes using {{nihongo|Kagami no Shio|鏡の塩}} sea salt.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Kagami Ceramics Fair'''|鏡陶磁器フェア|Kagami Tōjiki Fea}} — Biennial international ceramics exhibition (March), drawing approximately 90,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Kagami Bonito Festival'''|鏡鰹祭り|Kagami Katsuo Matsuri}} — Annual bonito festival (May), celebrating the start of the fishing season
* {{nihongo|'''Typhoon Thanksgiving'''|台風感謝祭|Taifū Kanshasai}} — Annual ceremony (November), giving thanks for surviving another typhoon season

